  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "B", dense: true, pad: 11.0pt)[
    Organic chemistry is the chemistry of the compounds of carbon, whether they come from a living thing or are made in a factory.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "C", dense: true, pad: 11.0pt)[
    Four bonds per atom and the ability to catenate — to form chains and rings of any length — are what give carbon its range.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "B", dense: true, pad: 11.0pt)[
    A homologous series shares a general formula and a functional group, and its members differ by a –CH₂– unit, so the properties change gradually.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "C", dense: true, pad: 11.0pt)[
    Every alkane is saturated, so it carries two hydrogens more than the alkene with the same number of carbons: CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "C", dense: true, pad: 11.0pt)[
    Saturated means that every carbon is bonded to four other atoms by single bonds, so no more hydrogen can be added.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "A", dense: true, pad: 11.0pt)[
    Methane is the first member of the alkane series: one carbon and 2(1) + 2 = 4 hydrogens.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "C", dense: true, pad: 11.0pt)[
    Butane has a straight chain and 2-methylpropane has a branch; there is no third arrangement of four carbons.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "B", dense: true, pad: 11.0pt)[
    Pentane, 2-methylbutane and 2,2-dimethylpropane — three in all.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "B", dense: true, pad: 11.0pt)[
    A longer chain has a larger surface area, so the van der Waals forces between molecules are stronger and more energy is needed to separate them.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "D", dense: true, pad: 11.0pt)[
    The two are isomers; branching makes the molecule compact, which reduces the surface contact and weakens the van der Waals forces.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "D", dense: true, pad: 11.0pt)[
    The top of the column is the coolest, so only the smallest molecules, the C₁–C₄ refinery gas, leave there as vapour.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "D", dense: true, pad: 11.0pt)[
    The bottom is the hottest part: the largest molecules never vaporise and are drawn off as the bitumen residue.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "B", dense: true, pad: 11.0pt)[
    The fractions are separated by boiling range in a fractionating column, which is fractional distillation on an industrial scale.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "A", dense: true, pad: 11.0pt)[
    Bitumen, the C₅₀-and-above residue, is the binder in road asphalt and in roofing felt.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "B", dense: true, pad: 11.0pt)[
    The C₁₀–C₁₄ fraction burns steadily in a wick or a jet engine, which is why it is the household and aviation fuel.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "B", dense: true, pad: 11.0pt)[
    Cracking breaks long chains into shorter ones, so a low-value heavy fraction becomes petrol, and the alkene produced feeds the polymer industry.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "C", dense: true, pad: 11.0pt)[
    The chain breaks to give a saturated short chain and an unsaturated one — an alkane plus an alkene.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "D", dense: true, pad: 11.0pt)[
    About 500 °C over a catalyst is enough to break the C–C bonds without carbonising the whole feed.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "A", dense: true, pad: 11.0pt)[
    Ultraviolet light splits Cl₂ into chlorine atoms, which attack the alkane: the reaction is a free-radical substitution.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "A", dense: true, pad: 11.0pt)[
    One hydrogen is replaced by one chlorine and HCl is released — a substitution, not an addition.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "A", dense: true, pad: 11.0pt)[
    Substitution continues until all four hydrogens have been replaced, so an excess of chlorine gives CCl₄.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "D", dense: true, pad: 11.0pt)[
    Only an unsaturated compound can add bromine; a saturated alkane leaves the orange colour unchanged.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "D", dense: true, pad: 11.0pt)[
    With enough oxygen every carbon becomes CO₂ and every hydrogen becomes H₂O.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "D", dense: true, pad: 11.0pt)[
    CO competes with oxygen for haemoglobin and is bound far more firmly, so the blood cannot carry oxygen; it is also colourless and odourless.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "A", dense: true, pad: 11.0pt)[
    Too little air leaves carbon particles glowing yellow and produces carbon monoxide instead of carbon dioxide.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "D", dense: true, pad: 11.0pt)[
    The octane number measures resistance to pre-ignition; branched alkanes and aromatics score high, straight chains low.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "C", dense: true, pad: 11.0pt)[
    Propane and butane liquefy under moderate pressure, which is why they can be sold in a cylinder.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "C", dense: true, pad: 11.0pt)[
    Natural gas is chiefly methane, with smaller amounts of ethane, propane and butane.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "A", dense: true, pad: 11.0pt)[
    C₂H₆ + 3½O₂ → 2CO₂ + 3H₂O, so the ratio is 1 : 3.5 and 10.0 cm³ needs 35.0 cm³ of oxygen.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "D", dense: true, pad: 11.0pt)[
    n(CH₄) = 8.0/16 = 0.50 mol, and 1 mol gives 1 mol of CO₂, so 0.50 × 44 = 22 g.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "A", dense: true, pad: 11.0pt)[
    The C–C and C–H bonds are strong and the molecules carry no charge, so water, acids and alkalis have nothing to attack.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "B", dense: true, pad: 11.0pt)[
    Paraffin means “little affinity”, given to the alkanes because they react with so few reagents.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "D", dense: true, pad: 11.0pt)[
    C₅H₁₀ fits CₙH₂ₙ, which is the alkene formula; every alkane must fit CₙH₂ₙ₊₂.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "C", dense: true, pad: 11.0pt)[
    For CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ the mass is 14n + 2; 14n + 2 = 72 gives n = 5, so the formula is C₅H₁₂.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "A", dense: true, pad: 11.0pt)[
    Chlorine can replace a hydrogen on carbon 1 or on carbon 2, giving 1-chloropropane and 2-chloropropane.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "B", dense: true, pad: 11.0pt)[
    Methane absorbs infrared radiation far more strongly than carbon dioxide — about 25 times as much per molecule.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "C", dense: true, pad: 11.0pt)[
    Same formula and different boiling points means a different arrangement of atoms: they are structural isomers of C₄H₁₀.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "D", dense: true, pad: 11.0pt)[
    The C₁–C₄ gases cannot be kept liquid at ordinary temperature without pressure, so they are used as fuel gas or as feedstock.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "A", dense: true, pad: 11.0pt)[
    Petroleum is the crude oil itself; petrol is one of the fractions distilled from it.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "C", dense: true, pad: 11.0pt)[
    C₄H₁₀ + 6½O₂ → 4CO₂ + 5H₂O, so 6.5 mol of oxygen are needed for each mole of butane.
  ]

]

